  "sn35.97:1.1": "“Negligence” (<i lang='pi' translate='no'>pamāda</i>) is from a root meaning to be mad or drunk. It’s often used in an ethical context, but here the Buddha extends it throughout the path. ",
  "sn35.97:1.2": "",
  "sn35.97:1.3": "",
  "sn35.97:1.4": "",
  "sn35.97:1.5": "The discourse links “negligence” with the process of mental uplifting that leads to <i lang='pi' translate='no'>samādhi</i> (see <a href='https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato#3.11'>SN 12.23:3.11</a>). ",
  "sn35.97:1.6": "",
  "sn35.97:1.7": "",
  "sn35.97:1.8": "",
  "sn35.97:1.9": "",
  "sn35.97:1.10": "“Principles” (<i lang='pi' translate='no'>dhammā</i>) such as the four noble truths, dependent origination, etc. ",
